-Because both pipelines spawn independent child processes (GNU~parallel in the legacy case; a spawn-based process pool in siOFF), the RSS reported reflects only the parent; aggregate memory across all 32 workers will exceed this figure.
+For each pipeline, one untimed warmup run on the 2{,}000-siRNA master set was executed and discarded to populate OS page-cache entries; then, for each batch size (500, 1{,}000, 2{,}000 siRNAs), five timed runs were executed, each with a fresh output directory.
+Wall-clock time was recorded with \texttt{/usr/bin/time -v} (GNU~time).
+Because both pipelines spawn independent child processes (GNU~parallel in the legacy case; a spawn-based process pool in siOFF), GNU~time's maximum resident-set size reflects only the single largest process ($\sim$8.8\,GB for legacy, $\sim$4.7\,GB for siOFF) rather than the run as a whole.
+Peak memory was therefore measured in every timed run by sampling the whole process tree every 0.5\,s and recording the peak of the summed proportional set size (PSS), in which a page shared by $N$ processes (e.g.\ a memory-mapped accessibility file) is charged $1/N$ to each.
 
 For batch sizes of 1{,}000 and 500, each of the five timed runs used an independently drawn random subset of the master 2{,}000-siRNA set, ensuring that variance estimates reflect genuine sampling variability rather than run-to-run measurement noise alone.
