-\subsection*{Accessibility pre-computation}
+The same pre-computed RIsearch2 binding-site prediction files were provided as input to both pipelines, so that search time is excluded from all measurements.
+The legacy pipeline reads these gzip-compressed files directly; for siOFF they were converted once to Parquet, the format RIsearch~(v3) emits natively, and this conversion is not timed.
+Likewise, siOFF memory-maps the per-chromosome accessibility arrays written by its \texttt{accessibility} subcommand (\cref{sec:accessibility-method}), analogous to the legacy pipeline's pre-computed binary accessibility files.
+Without accessibility, the off-targeting probabilities of the two pipelines agree for all 2{,}000 siRNAs of the master set (maximum absolute difference $3.9\times10^{-4}$).
+The results of this benchmark are presented in \cref{sec:pipeline-benchmark}.
 
-The legacy pipeline computed per-nucleotide RNA opening energies with a Python~2.7 script that wrapped the \texttt{RNAplfold} executable (ViennaRNA~2.6.3) as a subprocess, emitting two flat \texttt{uint8} binary files per chromosome (one per strand).
-siOFF's \texttt{accessibility} subcommand reproduces the same folding parameters ($W{=}80$, $L{=}40$, $u{=}30$) through the ViennaRNA~2.7.2 Python API directly, without spawning subprocesses: for each chromosome the call \texttt{RNA.fold\_compound(...).probs\_window(...)} returns per-position unpaired-probability vectors at the specified temperature (default $37\,^\circ$C, set via \texttt{-{}-temperature}), from which opening energies $O_i = -RT\ln p_i$ are computed in-process and written as a single per-chromosome Apache Parquet file with columnar compression and zero-copy Arrow reads.
+\subsection{Accessibility pre-computation}\label{sec:accessibility-method}
 
-\paragraph{Accessibility pre-computation benchmark.}
-Folding the complete GRCh38/hg38 reference ($W{=}80$, $L{=}40$, $u{=}30$, 64 workers on the AMD EPYC~9554 server) was compared between the legacy script (driven by GNU~\texttt{parallel}) and siOFF's \texttt{accessibility} subcommand (\cref{fig:accessibility}, \cref{tab:acc-bench}).
-siOFF completes the full-genome fold $\sim$34\% faster (8\,h\,46\,m vs.\ 13\,h\,12\,m) and stores the resulting profiles in $\sim$42\% less disk space (111.8\,GB vs.\ 192.6\,GB), the latter because each chromosome is written as a single two-strand compressed Parquet file rather than two uncompressed binary files.
-The trade-off is higher peak resident memory ($\sim$3.6\,GB vs.\ $\sim$309\,MB, roughly $12\times$), as the ViennaRNA Python API holds per-chromosome probability matrices in memory rather than streaming them through a subprocess; both figures are small relative to typical server memory, and this cost is incurred only once per genome and folding condition.
+The legacy pipeline computed per-nucleotide RNA opening energies with a Python~2.7 script that wrapped the \texttt{RNAplfold} executable (ViennaRNA~2.5.1) as a subprocess, emitting two flat \texttt{uint8} binary files per chromosome (one per strand).
+siOFF's \texttt{accessibility} subcommand reproduces the same folding parameters ($W{=}80$, $L{=}40$, $u{=}30$) through the ViennaRNA~2.7.2 Python API directly, without spawning subprocesses: for each chromosome the call \texttt{RNA.fold\_compound(...).probs\_window(...)} returns per-position unpaired-probability vectors at the specified temperature (default $37\,^\circ$C, set via \texttt{-{}-temperature}), from which opening energies $O_i = -RT\ln p_i$ are computed in-process and written, for each chromosome and strand, as a \texttt{uint8} NumPy array of opening energies $\times 10$ (one byte per position and unpaired-region length, as in the legacy files), with a sidecar holding the exact values of entries above 25.5\,kcal/mol; the off-target stage memory-maps these arrays without conversion.
+The corresponding full-genome benchmark is reported in \cref{sec:accessibility-benchmark}.
 
